/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (This particular system is not new.  It shares some similarities with the way Single Sign-on \(SSO\) logins function on the Internet. In that case, to authenticate oneself with a service provider, the user must present proof of their identity using credentials provided by Google or Facebook \(the issuers\), while the service provider acts as the verifier. However, the key difference between this model and ZK ID is in the way the parties involved interact with each other, and how data is managed and processed.)
/E (This particular system is not new.  It shares some similarities with the way Single Sign-on \(SSO\) logins function on the Internet. In that case, to authenticate oneself with a service provider, the user must present proof of their identity using credentials provided by Google or Facebook \(the issuers\), while the service provider acts as the verifier. However, the key difference between this model and ZK ID is in the way the parties involved interact with each other, and how data is managed and processed.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
