<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (In most ZK ID systems, the credentials are owned by the ID Holder and stored in their wallet. This allows the ID Holder to have complete control over their identity. Furthermore, when the ID Holder is proving their identity to a verifier, the privacy-preserving capabilities of ZKPs ensure that no information is shared. Instead, the proof simply confirms the validity of the credential.)
/E (In most ZK ID systems, the credentials are owned by the ID Holder and stored in their wallet. This allows the ID Holder to have complete control over their identity. Furthermore, when the ID Holder is proving their identity to a verifier, the privacy-preserving capabilities of ZKPs ensure that no information is shared. Instead, the proof simply confirms the validity of the credential.)
/Pg 14 0 R
